          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            %strong spectrum
            is a geometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            constructed from some algebraic, category-theoretic, analytic or similar data which typically do not have an obvious/manifest geometric meaning.
          %p Examples are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+spectrum"} Gelfand spectrum
                of a commutative
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebra"} C*-algebra
            %li
              %p
                the spectrum of a commutative
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
                is an enhanced
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurable+space"} measurable space
            %li
              %p
                the Grothendieck
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+of+a+commutative+ring"} spectrum of a commutative ring
            %li
              %p
                the formal spectrum of a complete noetherian
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            %li
              %p
                the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  primitive spectrum
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/primitive+spectrum"} ?
                of a noncommutative (but unital) ring,
            %li
              %p
                the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  left spectrum
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/left+spectrum"} ?
                of a noncommutative ring,
            %li
              %p
                the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Rosenberg spectrum
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Rosenberg+spectrum"} ?
                of an
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel+spectrum"} Gabriel spectrum
                of indecomposable injectives,
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierce+spectrum"} Pierce spectrum
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berkovich+spectrum"} Berkovich spectrum
                in rigid
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+geometry"} analytic geometry
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diers+spectrum"} Diers spectrum
                recovers many of the above constructions,
            %li
              %p …
          %p One sometimes says “spectrum” also for the underlying sets of such geometric spectra.
          %p
            A spectrum does not necessarily give a faithful representation of the original data. For example, the Gelfand spectrum of a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reconstruction"} reconstruction
            theorem), but is not a sufficient invariant if we consider all
            = succeed "-algebras." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
          %p
            The word ‘spectrum’ in this setup originates from the fact that the spectrum of a commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+algebra"} Banach algebra
            is a natural extension of the theory of a spectrum of a family of commuting self-adjoint operators, which is in turn the generalization of the spectral theory of one self-adjoint operator. See also
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                spectrum of a Banach algebra
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/spectrum+of+a+Banach+algebra"} ?
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spectrum"} Wikipedia
